🧠 Step 4: The Service (MANUAL MAPPING HERE) ✍️
This is where the change happens. No ModelMapper. Just Java code.
package org.example.demo1.service;

import org.example.demo1.dto.UserRequest;
import org.example.demo1.dto.UserResponse;
import org.example.demo1.model.UserEntity;
import org.example.demo1.repository.UserRepo;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class UserService {

    @Autowired
    private UserRepo userRepo;

    public UserResponse createUser(UserRequest request) {

        // --- STEP 1: Request DTO -> Entity ---
        // We create the entity manually
        UserEntity entity = new UserEntity();
        
        // We manually copy every field
        entity.setName(request.getName());
        entity.setEmail(request.getEmail());
        entity.setPassword(request.getPassword());

        // --- STEP 2: Save to DB ---
        UserEntity savedEntity = userRepo.save(entity);

        // --- STEP 3: Entity -> Response DTO ---
        // We create the response manually
        UserResponse response = new UserResponse();
        
        // We manually copy fields we WANT to show
        response.setId(savedEntity.getId());
        response.setName(savedEntity.getName());
        response.setEmail(savedEntity.getEmail());
        
        // Note: We intentionally DO NOT copy the password here.
        // response.setPassword(...) <--- This line doesn't exist!

        return response;
    }
}

🚦 Step 5: The Controller
Stays exactly the same. The Controller doesn't care HOW the mapping happened.
package org.example.demo1.controller;

import org.example.demo1.dto.UserRequest;
import org.example.demo1.dto.UserResponse;
import org.example.demo1.service.UserService;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.PostMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RequestBody;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;

@RestController
public class UserController {

    @Autowired
    private UserService userService;

    @PostMapping("/users")
    public ResponseEntity<UserResponse> createUser(@RequestBody UserRequest request) {
        UserResponse response = userService.createUser(request);
        return new ResponseEntity<>(response, HttpStatus.CREATED);
    }
}

🏆 Which one is better?
 * Manual (This code): Faster execution, easier to debug, no external libraries. Preferred by big tech companies.
 * ModelMapper: Less code to write, but harder to debug if something goes wrong.
Since you are learning, use this Manual version. It forces you to understand exactly what data goes where.
